The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
if (line == null) return null; // end of input       
```
```java
if (reader != null) {
    IOUtils.closeQuietly(reader);
}
```
Reason:
The first statement does not clean up resources when the end of input is reached, leading to potential resource leaks. The second statement in the `destroy` method also fails to ensure that resources are properly closed when the `LineEntityProcessor` is done processing, which can result in improper state management.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (line == null) {
    closeResources();
    return null;
}
```
```java
public void closeResources() {
    if (reader != null) {
        IOUtils.closeQuietly(reader);
    }
    reader = null;
}
```
Reason:
The first statement now calls `closeResources()` when the end of input is reached, ensuring that resources are properly cleaned up. The `closeResources()` method centralizes the resource cleanup logic, making it easier to manage and ensuring that the `reader` is set to `null` after closing, preventing further access to a potentially closed resource.

